> Within this economic environment, a regression that shows how the marginal effects of weather
> A-12
> vary cross-sectionally with climate thereby yields how the total costs of adaptation accrue over time
> as a location’s climate changes.
> The key assumption is the timeless nature of the decision-making environment. It has two
> implications. First, optimizing agents equate the flow of marginal costs to the flow of marginal
> benefits at every instant, so that instantaneous marginal costs can be substituted pointwise for
> instantaneous marginal benefits (equation (A-12)). Second, adaptation to different climates over
> time is equivalent to observed cross-sectional adaptation to different climates over space (equa-
> tion (A-14)). If adaptation were instead not completely transitory, optimization would not permit
> problem (A-11) to be solved via (A-12) and there is little reason to expect (A-14) to hold.
